&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Kenneth J. Arrow Lecture Series&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;a href="https://cup.columbia.edu/series/kenneth-j-arrow-lecture-series"&gt;The Kenneth J. Arrow Lecture Series&lt;/a&gt; highlights economists, from Nobel laureates to groundbreaking younger scholars, whose work builds on Kenneth J. Arrow&amp;rsquo;s scholarship as well as his innovative spirit. The books in the series are an expansion of the lectures that are held in Arrow&amp;rsquo;s honor at Columbia University.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Institute of Global Politics (IGP) at Columbia SIPA&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
The Institute of Global Politics (IGP) convenes leading scholars and practitioners to advance policy solutions to today&amp;rsquo;s pressing global challenges. IGP brings together experts&amp;mdash;from the public and private sectors and across the political spectrum&amp;mdash;to foster civil discourse and create evidence-based policy strategies for local to global impact.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Center for Political Economy at Columbia World Projects&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Launched in 2022, the Columbia Center for Political Economy has two primary goals: to identify and advance the most promising post-2008 developments within economics and to promote a new political economy with robust philosophical underpinnings. This approach is distinctive for our time, with an institutional, cross-disciplinary orientation connecting economics to history, law, political science, sociology, public health, engineering, data science and more.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Program for Economic Research at Columbia&amp;rsquo;s Department of Economics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Founded in 2003, the Program for Economic Research (PER) at Columbia University focuses on identifying and funding research aimed at the economic challenges, trends, and policies affecting people around the world, informing our understanding of global economic issues. PER&amp;rsquo;s diverse portfolio of academic programs and initiatives combines theory with practical application, emphasizing open access and active learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;REGISTER HERE&lt;/strong&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.google.com/url?q=https%3A%2F%2Fbit.ly%2FCGT_Eurozone&amp;amp;sa=D&amp;amp;ust=1740864720000000&amp;amp;usg=AOvVaw3d-2HHk0nEjsK9-byMvQD_" target="_blank"&gt;https://bit.ly/CGT_Eurozone&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Join the Committee on Global Thought, the European Institute, and the Center for Political Economy to explore the challenges facing the Euro area and the prospects for its future development.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Europe&amp;#39;s common currency, the Euro, is entering its second quarter century. The European Central Bank is firmly established as the #2 central bank in the world. It has survived years of turbulence. For now, debt markets are stable and the politics of Europe&amp;#39;s common currency are quiet. Can this last?&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Europe faces huge challenges. Its economy is sluggish. Europe fears falling behind in the global tech race. It faces new security threats. Trans-Atlantic relations have been thrown into turmoil by the reelection of Donald Trump. On the horizon looms the challenge of the energy transition. This panel will explore the challenges facing the Euro area and the prospects for its future development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;On March 11, we&amp;#39;re bringing together voices from Latin American politics, civil society, and the university for a fresh conversation on&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Women and Democracy in Latin America&lt;/strong&gt;. Across the Western Hemisphere,&amp;nbsp;organizations and movements have emerged that are&amp;nbsp;focused on issues facing women in the public square, themes like leadership development, representation in politics, and online violence. What do these initiatives signal about changes in the broader political landscape and the emergence of new political&amp;nbsp;leadership? How do they interact with traditional political structures and&amp;nbsp;forms of participation? What is the promise of this new wave of civic participation for democratic resilience across the region?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Join us for a discussion which features reflections on these questions and others, drawn from experiences of leaders and experts on the region. Register &lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/women-and-democracy-in-latin-america-tickets-1259546390159?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

