&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for an opening reception featuring the exhibit&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;An infinite archive&lt;/em&gt;, by Elidor M&amp;euml;hilli.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since the early 2000s, Elidor M&amp;euml;hilli has assembled a vast archive on the dramatic transformation of his hometown, Tirana, Albania. This body of work encompasses photographic series, thousands of documents, ephemera, and found objects. While research often takes him inside formal party, state, and security police archives, this broader visual practice reimagines the idea of the archive itself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;M&amp;euml;hilli&amp;rsquo;s work captures Tirana&amp;rsquo;s bewildering transition from communist-era central planning to neoliberalism. The collapse of the party-state in the early 1990s triggered a mass migration to the periphery of the city, which was ill-equipped to handle it. Ordinary residents transformed into amateur architects, converting ground floors into shops and adding illegal floors to prefabricated blocks. Various governments have tried to dominate the city, but Tirana tends to defy them, pursuing an unruled life.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As tourism surges today, star architects flock to the capital to design gleaming high-rises. The construction craze and the steep rise in real estate prices puts immense pressure on historical landmarks located on valuable land. In this heady rush to attract money and attention&amp;mdash;to reinvent a city and a country so that it becomes legible to the West&amp;mdash;what to do with versions of the past deemed &lt;em&gt;unmarketable&lt;/em&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a process that began with earlier shows at galleries at Cornell University (2005) and Princeton University (2007), M&amp;euml;hilli explores this ongoing tug-of-war. He tracks the transformation of familiar places of his teenage years, like the raw graffitied fa&amp;ccedil;ade of a housing block that served as a backdrop to a playground and a mysterious kiosk stuffed with old books that has somehow survived the decades of turmoil. He assembles textual and visual biographies of erased architecture, documents the denials to his requests for access to classified archival files, collects &amp;ldquo;obsolete history&amp;rdquo; discarded along city streets, and writes editorials for local papers, putting together a counter-archive to a country struggling with its past and desperate to plug itself into global circuits of capital.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Navigating HOT Moments: Before, During, &amp;amp; After Class (for Faculty)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A 60-minute workshop for Columbia Faculty.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Regardless of course topic or content,&amp;nbsp;HOT&amp;mdash;heated, offensive, or tense&amp;mdash;moments in the classroom are always a possibility. Though never fully avoidable or predictable, there are steps instructors can take to help mitigate the potential for these moments, and strategies to help better equip instructors for navigating HOT moments when they occur. This session will help instructors identify strategies for engaging with, facilitating, and navigating HOT moments before, during, and after class.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unable to join us for this workshop? Engage with the CTL on-demand resource: &lt;a href="https://ctl.columbia.edu/resources-and-technology/resources/navigating-hot-moments/" target="_blank"&gt;Navigating Heated, Offensive, and Tense (HOT) Moments in the Classroom&lt;/a&gt;. You can also schedule a &lt;a href="https://ctl.columbia.edu/faculty/offerings/consultations/" target="_blank"&gt;consultation&lt;/a&gt;, or find other workshops on our &lt;a href="https://ctl.columbia.edu/events/" target="_blank"&gt;CTL events calendar&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Columbia University makes every effort to accommodate individuals with disabilities. Contact ColumbiaCTL@columbia.edu or 212.854.1692 for accommodations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Human Rights Institute&amp;nbsp;proudly presents our Spring&amp;nbsp;event series on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Activism Against Authoritarianism&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This series is co-sponsored by:&amp;nbsp;Columbia Law Students Human Rights Association, Columbia Journal of Transnational Law, and the Columbia Human Rights Law Review&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&amp;nbsp;invite&amp;nbsp;you to join us at our fourth event&amp;nbsp;for a discussion&amp;nbsp;with:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mandeep Tiwana, Interim Co-Secretary General, CIVICUS&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Topic:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Nearly 75 % of the world&amp;#39;s population lives in countries with closed or repressed civic space. How do authoritarian governments repress, and what are recent global civic space trends, during this period of resurgent authoritarianism and nationalism? How do political and economic elites collude to backslide on rights and democracy? And, how are activists around the world fighting back? Join us for a conversation with Mandeep Tiwana, Interim Co-Secretary General at CIVICUS, to learn how activists are successfully advancing rights from Poland to Bangladesh.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Wednesday, March 26, 2025 | 12:10pm - 1:10pm&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Columbia Law School | Jerome Greene Annex&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please register below.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Liza Pon, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Associate Director for Shared Resources (SRs)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dept. of Pathology &amp;amp; Cell Biology&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title: &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Overview of Confocal and Specialized Microscopy Shared Resource (CSMSR)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Description:&amp;nbsp;Liza Pon, PhD, Associate Director for Shared Resources (SRs) will share an overview of Confocal and Specialized Microscopy Shared Resource (CSMSR). CSMSR offers advanced microscope systems for high-resolution, multidimensional optical imaging of living and fixed cells and tissues. It provides access to state-of-the-art confocal microscopes, specialized imaging equipment and expert assistance in image analysis and presentation. CSMSR is dedicated to facilitating high-resolution imaging studies, enabling researchers to visualize cellular and molecular processes critical to understanding cancer biology.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Remi Creusot, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Faculty Director of FCSR&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dept. of Med Hematology and Oncology&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title:&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Overview of Flow Cytometry Shared Resource (FCSR)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Description:&amp;nbsp;Remi Creusot, PhD, Faculty Director of the Flow Cytometry Shared Resource (FCSR) will provide an overview of FCSR. FCSR, in partnership with the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center and the Columbia Center for Translational Immunology, offers researchers training and access to advanced flow cytometry technologies. It provides support for experimental design, data analysis, and various applications, such as cell characterization based on surface proteins, cytokines, transcription factors, and signaling molecules. It also provides cell sorting services for a wide range of downstream applications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
